--TEST--
The six ways a declaration is asked for something it does not do: a second declaration of a live name, an assignment to a name that was never declared, a value of another type, a read on a path no assignment reaches, a bare array literal under `var`, and `settype` (`rule:types/declaration`, `rule:types/var-inference`, `rule:types/conversion`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/every-binding-has-a-type

// `rule:types/declaration`: a binding is declared once, and a second
// declaration of a live name names the first one rather than shadowing it.
int $count = 1;
int $count = 2;

// `rule:types/declaration`: every later assignment is bare, and is legal only
// where the name is already declared in the enclosing function.
$never = 3;

// `rule:types/declaration`: the declared type is fixed, so a value of another
// type is refused where it is written.
$count = "two";

// `rule:types/declaration`: definite assignment is checked, where PHP warns
// and hands back `null`.
int $unset;
echo $unset, "\n";

// `rule:types/var-inference`: an array literal is checked against a target
// rather than inferring one, so `var` has nothing to read its element type
// from.
var $xs = [1, 2, 3];

// `rule:types/conversion`: PHP's one way of retyping a live binding is
// refused, naming the conversion that makes a new one instead.
settype($count, "string");
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0406]: `$count` is already declared
%A
error[E0301]: `$never` is assigned to but was never declared
%A
error[E0401]: expected `int`, found `string`
%A
error[E0301]: `$unset` is read before any assignment reaches it
%A
error[E0414]: `var` cannot infer an array literal's element type
%A
error[E0208]: `settype` is not supported
%A
error: aborting due to 6 errors
